WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.080
Feifei: Welcome to The English We Speak.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.740
It's Feifei here with Rob, and we're in a bit

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:14.820
of a rush…

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.160
Rob: …yes yes, I've hurt my foot and we've

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.720
got to get to hospital. Ouch!

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:23.040
Feifei: Ooo, it looks nasty Rob – all red

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.840
and swollen. It was a good job I was

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:27.330
driving by when you hurt it.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.869
Rob: Yes yes, thanks for the lift, Feifei. But it

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:32.509
really is hurting, so could you step on it,

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:33.199
please?

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:34.199
Feifei: What?

00:00:34.199 --> 00:00:35.530
Rob: Step on it!

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:39.260
Feifei: Oh, OK. If you say so.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:43.260
Rob: Ow! That was painful – what did you

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:44.460
do that for?

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.280
Feifei: You said 'step on it' – I thought that

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.670
was an odd request, but I did.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:53.180
Rob: No, Feifei! I meant drive faster –

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:55.440
that's what 'step on it' means. It's what

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.760
you say to someone to ask them to hurry.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:00.100
That hurt so much.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.740
Feifei: Oops, sorry! Right, well hold on

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.280
tight, Rob. I will step on it while we hear

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:07.300
some examples…

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:14.680
Taxi! Could you take me to the

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:17.940
station, please? And could step on it? I'm late!

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.139
Our coach told us to step on it if we

00:01:20.139 --> 00:01:22.210
wanted to get to the match on time.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.680
Please step on it, otherwise we're going to

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:25.600
miss the plane.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.880
Feifei: This is The English We Speak from

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.540
BBC Learning English. And we're finding

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.000
out about the phrase 'step on it', which

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.880
means 'go faster' or 'hurry up'.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.800
Well, we're nearly at the hospital, Rob.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.100
Rob: Good good, because my foot hurts

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.840
so much – I need urgent medical

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.100
attention.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.120
Feifei: How exactly did you hurt it?

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:52.880
Rob: I was playing football and another

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:56.480
player stepped on my big toe. It was so painful.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:59.220
Feifei: What! So you've got me to 'step on

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.600
it' – and drive at high speed – because

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.980
someone stepped on your big toe?

00:02:03.987 --> 00:02:08.340
This isn't an emergency, Rob - you can get out and walk.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:09.690
Rob: That's not very nice.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.732
Feifei: You had better step on it, Rob –

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:14.060
the casualty department shuts in five

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.000
minutes. Bye.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:17.020
Rob: Bye.

